LSH-256 and LSH-512 are the hash functions of the Korean standard KS X 3262. We present shimeji, an implementation of both in pure Rust, and compare it with eight other implementations in C, C++, Go and Rust on an Apple M6 and an Intel Core i9-13900K. On the M6, shimeji runs the steps of the compression function on the integer units while NEON expands the message schedule. On the i9, it holds the state in SSE or AVX2 registers. The lane orders of these registers keep word permutations off the critical path of the step, and its LSH-512 code moves words across 128-bit lanes in only half of the steps. As a geometric mean over messages from 16 bytes to 1 MiB, shimeji hashes one message of LSH-256 and LSH-512 #r2(geo("m6", "lsh256")) and #r2(geo("m6", "lsh512")) times as fast as KISA's C code, the fastest other implementation on the M6. On long messages it takes #r2(cpb("m6", "lsh256", big, "shimeji")) and #r2(cpb("m6", "lsh512", big, "shimeji")) cycles per byte and retires #pct(fewer("lsh256")) and #pct(fewer("lsh512")) fewer instructions than KISA's C code. On the i9, its geometric-mean speedup over the fastest other implementation at each length is #r2(geo("i9", "lsh256")) for LSH-256 and #r2(geo("i9", "lsh512")) for LSH-512, and it takes #r2(cpb("i9", "lsh256", big, "shimeji")) and #r2(cpb("i9", "lsh512", big, "shimeji")) cycles per byte on long messages. Its streaming hasher keeps #measured("m6", "state", "lsh256", "shimeji") and #measured("m6", "state", "lsh512", "shimeji") bytes of state, as many as KISA's, and uses less stack than any variant of KISA's code.#for m in ("m6", "i9") { for fam in ("lsh256", "lsh512") { assert(measured(m, "stack", fam, "shimeji") < kisa-least-stack(m, fam)) } }
